syslog
-----Start-----
Each program(haproxy, ngnix etc) it wont directly write log to file, instead it send via unix socket /dev/log and it contain logs from that place syslog take the logs and based on syslog configuration rules it will write to file.
program syslog config rules file present inside  /etc/rsyslog.conf and /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf. It will be auto created when program installed or admin will create it.
Below is the rule for haproxy rule inside /etc/rsyslog.d/. When haproxy run , syslog collect the log and save to filke based on below rules

$AddUnixListenSocket /var/lib/haproxy/dev/log - Since ha proxy run in change root, o it will create one unix socket.

# Send HAProxy messages to a dedicated logfile
Here if the logfile on unixsocket contain with program name haproxy those log will save to haproxy.log in path mentioned below once stored it will stop it so it wont write to other files.
:programname, startswith, "haproxy" {
  /var/log/haproxy.log
  stop
}
in this location /etc/rsyslog.d/if it has many rules , each rule has number start with, so when syslog looking at rule in order,the first order with rule match there it will write log ie in that ath mentioned in that rule
