awk & sed
-----Start-----
Using awk we can split the lines. By default it will delimit by space, we can use custom  delimit by using -F'"', here delimit by ". After delimit each filed will be start with $1 which means first field. '{print $2}' gies us second filed.
What we do below is first we grep all the logfile end with http/1 which meand proper http response file, then op we passed to awk and split by " then taking second filed then that op we split by ? then taking first field the sort with alphabetical order , then uniq -c, if two identical word are there like a a next to eachother in rows it willad as one and make count as 2 then again sort with -nr means n means numerical then with r means decending prder
grep -i "http/1" haproxy-aug6.log | awk -F'"' '{print $2}'| awk -F'?' '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | grep -i 'api'

Here sed - stream editor, it reads all the lines in file by line by line and perform operation like serrch, replace, print and delete and -E means friendly writing pattern.
On sed we need to write the req ex and finding that and replacing
Here  sed -E 's/.*:([0-9]{2}):[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}\..*/\1/' the syntax is 's/pattern/\1/' - here .*:([0-9]{2}):[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}\..* we are trying to get data from this timestamp [27/Jul/2026:09:15:02.456].
first .* say sequence of any character
. means "any single character."
.* means "any number of any characters" (greedy — grabs as much as it can).
: matches a literal colon.
[0-9] means "any single digit, 0 through 9."
{2} means "exactly two of whatever came right before it" — so [0-9]{2} means "exactly two digits."
(...) — parentheses mark a capturing group: "remember whatever matched inside here, I want to use it later."
\. — a literal dot character (a plain . would normally mean "any character," so the backslash tells sed "no, I actually mean a dot here").
Put together, this pattern matches a full timestamp and specifically remembers the middle HH (hour) portion in its one capturing group.
s/PATTERN/\1/ — the substitution instruction: replace everything that matched the whole pattern with just \1, which means "whatever was captured in group 1" — i.e., throw away everything else and keep only the hour.
grep -i "http/1" haproxy-aug6.log | awk '{print $7}' | sed -E 's/.*:([0-9]{2}):[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}\..*/\1/' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr

Command to filter the log based on the range and save it to new file
Sed- stream editor , its used to read all the lines in a file line by line and perform operation like search, replace, print and delete for lines.
How to filter the file using sed
sed -n '/start/,/end/p'  file- here -n refers only print the line which match the pattern, if we dont give -n for every line sed read that also print then resulted output also it print so duplicated data, then since we use -n so print the resulted lines use page

sed -n '/^Aug 10 07:59:00/,/^Aug 10 09:10:00/p' file > resulted_file
Here we are filtering the data based on time range. Here ^ refer if the line start with Jul then only start match and same for ^Aug, it start with Aug then till that collect and end it. if it start with info Jul it wont match that

